  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/gone-forever/merchants-hotel/708-screenshot-2021-09-02-120726.jpg" alt="Merchants Hotel in an 1883 bird's-eye view of Mandan">
    <figcaption>Excerpt from Stoner's 1883 bird's-eye view of Mandan</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>

<p>Henry and Philomena Yunck moved with their two-year-old daughter, Marie, to Bismarck, Dakota Territory, in 1878. They lived at the steamboat landing and operated a boardinghouse. Philomena prepared meals for the bridge-construction crew, while Henry worked as a laborer at the landing. In 1879, the couple and their children, now including son Joseph, moved across the river to Mandan and opened the hotel the following January.</p>

<p>Philomena became known for her meals. A ten-cent breakfast included hotcakes, sausage or bacon, fruit, and unlimited coffee; dinners cost no more than 65 cents. She prepared meals for the {% person "marquis-de-mores" %}Marquis de Morès{% endperson %} while he was jailed behind the hotel awaiting trial for murder. {% person "spotted-elk" %}Chief Si Tanka{% endperson %}—also known as Spotted Elk or Big Foot—frequently dined with the Yuncks while traveling to and from Cannonball. {% person "theodore-roosevelt" %}Theodore Roosevelt{% endperson %} was a familiar figure at the hotel during early-1880s trips between Bismarck and Medora, when he stopped to hunt ducks with friends. {% person "grover-cleveland" %}Grover Cleveland{% endperson %} also appears in the 1884 hotel register.</p>

<p>Countless immigrants relocating to Dakota Territory stayed without registering. The Yuncks often provided them free meals and lodging, eventually erecting a separate building at the rear of the property for that purpose.</p>

